We sincerely thank all reviewers for the constructive feedbacks. 

Specifically, we appreciate the reviewer's recognition of our contributions, including 

* the novelty of MoVT framework and its practical usefulness (reviewer `kCu9`, `n9fj`, `NdeY`,  `Yq9o`);
* the effective design of two stage AdaVaR training pipeline and the AdaGRPO algorithm (reviewer `n9fj`, `NdeY`, `Yq9o`);
* the thorough and comprehensive experiment analysis, exhibiting robust and cross‑domain improvements (reviewer  `kCu9`, `n9fj`, `NdeY`, `Yq9o`)
* the clear and well-organized presentation of our paper (reviewer `NdeY`, `Yq9o`).

Based on the feedbacks from the first round, we have revised our paper. Below is an overview of the revisions made (W and Q are short for weakness and question, respectively):

1.  We have **refined Appendix A to provide additional details on the implementation details**:

    * We have refined **Appendix A.2** to better **clarify the data construction process**, in which:
      1.  In A.2.1, we add discussion on data copyright compliance in Lines 791-795 (for W8 & Q6 of Reviewer `n9fj`), and includ additional description on data quality checks in Lines 797-800 (for W15 & Q13 of Reviewer `n9fj`);

      2.  In A.2.2, the "Difficulty-based Filtering" paragraph has been added with details and the rationale for the filtering threshold selection (for W14 & Q12 of Reviewer `n9fj`);

      3.  In A.2.2, the "Multi-Modal Data Processing" paragraph has been added with a detailed description of the data processing methods (for W20 & Q18 of Reviewer `n9fj`);

      4.  In A.2.2, the "Summary on data efficiency" paragraph has been added with a summarized discussion of the data sources and quantities (for W27 & Q25 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

    * We have refined **Appendix A.3** by adding several paragraphs to provide further details on the **training parameter settings and their justifications**, including:
      * Number of training epochs for SFT (for W29 & Q27 of Reviewer `n9fj`)
      * Number of rollouts generated per sample during RL (for W30 & Q28 of Reviewer `n9fj`)
      * Coefficient for KL penalty (for W2 & Q2 of Reviewer `n9fj`)

    * **Appendix A.4** has also been expanded with **additional evaluation details**, including:
      * In the "Average Schema" paragraph, we add clarification on the schema for averaging across benchmarks (for W28 & Q26 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In the "Mode-Switching Mechanism" paragraph, we provide further explanation of the mode-switching mechanism (for W6 & Q3 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In the "Compared Models" paragraph, we include implementation details of the compared models (for W5 & Q4 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In the "Evaluation Environment and Cost" paragraph, we add details on the hardware environment used for evaluation (for W31 & Q29 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

We sincerely appreciate your constructive feedbacks. We have uploaded an updated version. Below is an overview of the revisions made (W and Q are short for weakness and question, respectively):

1.  We have refined Appendix A to provide additional details on the implementation details of our method:

    * We have refined Appendix A.2 to better clarify the data construction process, in which:
      1.  In A.2.1, we add discussion on copyright compliance during the VoCoT reconstruction process in Lines 791-795 (for W8 & Q6), and includ additional description on quality checks of the reconstructed data in Lines 797-800 (for W15 & Q13);

      2.  In A.2.2, the "Difficulty-based Filtering" paragraph has been added with details on the difficulty-based filtering method and the rationale for the threshold selection (for W14 & Q12);

      3.  In A.2.2, the "Multi-Modal Data Processing" paragraph has been added with a detailed description of the data processing methods (for W20 & Q18);

      4.  In A.2.2, the "Summary on data efficiency" paragraph has been added with a summary and discussion of the data sources and quantities (for W27 & Q25).

    * We have refined Appendix A.3 by adding several paragraphs to provide further details on the training parameter settings and their justifications, including:
      * Number of training epochs for SFT (for W29 & Q27)
      * Number of rollouts generated per sample during RL (for W30 & Q28)
      * Coefficient for KL penalty (for W2 & Q2)

    * Appendix A.4 has also been expanded with additional details, including:
      * In the "Average Schema" paragraph, we add clarification on the schema for averaging across benchmarks (for W28 & Q26);
      * In the "Mode-Switching Mechanism" paragraph, we provide explanations of the mode-switching mechanism (for W6 & Q3);
      * In the "Compared Models" paragraph, we include implementation details of the compared models (for W5 & Q4);
      * In the "Evaluation Environment and Cost" paragraph, we add details on the hardware environment used for evaluation (for W31 & Q29).

2.  We have carefully revised Appendix B to provide a comprehensive discussion:

    * We have expanded the original Appendix B.1 into a thorough analysis of mode selection:

      * In Appendix B.1.2, we add an analysis of mode selection across different image types (for W37 & Q35);
      * In Appendix B.1.3, we include attention-based visualizations to analyze the mode selection process (for W36 & Q34);
      * In Appendix B.1.4, we compare the mode selection strategy learned by AdaGRPO with simple mode ensemble strategies (for W36 & Q34).

    * We add a new section, Appendix B.3, to conduct a comprehensive error analysis (for Q40).
    * We add a new section, Appendix B.4, to analyze computational efficiency, including:
      * In B.4.1, a detailed comparison of training efficiency between GRPO and AdaGRPO (for W12 & Q10);
      * In B.4.2, an evaluation verifying the efficient inference performance of AdaVaR (for W13 & Q11).
    * The original Appendix B.3 is expanded to Appendix B.5 in the revised version: supplementing the training loss curve of the SFT stage (for W29 & Q27) and the rollout length curves of the two modes during RL (for W38 & Q36).
    * We have refined the original Appendix B.4 (now updated as Appendix B.6) to discuss the scalability of our method and directions for future work:
      * B.6.1 discusses the extensibility of MoVT to more and more complex reasoning modes (for W11 & Q9);
      * B.6.2 discusses the scalability of the AdaVaR training pipeline (for W11 & Q9);
      * B.6.3 discusses extensions to more modalities (for W34 & Q32) and languages (for W40 & Q38)

    * We have added a new section, Appendix B.7, to present supplementary ablation studies:

      * In B.7.1, we validate the rationale for using a 1:1 ratio of the two reasoning modes in SFT (for W1 & Q1);
      * In B.7.2, we show that different formats of the mode prefix have negligible impact on the MoVT framework (for W3);
      * In B.7.4, we verify the necessity of the easy-to-hard data ordering for curriculum learning (for W35 & Q33);
      * In B.7.5, we demonstrate the robustness of our method to image noise (for W21 & Q19).

3.  We have streamlined the citations that originally contained excessively long author lists (for W23 & Q21).

4.  We have added clarification in Algorithm 1 regarding the order of the reward list (for W25 & Q23).

5.  We have included a link to the anonymous codebase in the reproducibility statement (for W9 & Q7).

6.  To avoid disrupting the numbering of figures and prevent confusion, new figures are currently appended at the end of the submission version. We will reorganize and renumber them appropriately after the review process is completed.

Above all, thank you very much for your detailed suggestions, which have greatly helped improve the quality of this paper. We also warmly welcome any further feedback or recommendations.

2.  We have carefully **revised Appendix B to provide a comprehensive supplementary analysis and discussion**:

    * We have **expanded** the original **Appendix B.1** into **a comprehensive analysis of mode selection** (for W3 & Q3 of Reviewer `kCu9`, W5 & Q5 of Reviewer `Yq9o`):

      * In Appendix B.1.2, we add an analysis of mode selection across different image types (for W37 & Q35 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In Appendix B.1.3, we include attention-based visualizations to analyze the mode selection process (for W36 & Q34 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In Appendix B.1.4, we compare the mode selection strategy learned by AdaGRPO with simple mode ensemble strategies (for W36 & Q34 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

    * We **add** **a new section, Appendix B.3**, to conduct **a comprehensive error analysis** (for Q2 of Reviewer `Yq9o`, Q40 of Reviewer `n9fj`).
    * We **supplement a new section, Appendix B.4**, to analyze **computational efficiency**, including:
      * In B.4.1, a detailed comparison of training efficiency between GRPO and AdaGRPO (for W3 of Reviewer `Yq9o`, W12 & Q10 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In B.4.2, an evaluation verifying the efficient inference performance of AdaVaR's adaptive mode reasoning (for W13 & Q11 of Reviewer `n9fj`).
    * The original Appendix B.3 is expanded to **Appendix B.5** in the revised version: supplementing the **training loss curve of the SFT stage** (for W29 & Q27 of Reviewer `n9fj`) and the **rollout length curves** of the two modes **during RL** (for W38 & Q36 of Reviewer `n9fj`).
    * We have refined the original Appendix B.4 (now updated as **Appendix B.6** in the latest version) to **discuss the scalability of our method and directions for future work**:
      * B.6.1 discusses the extensibility of the MoVT paradigm to more and more complex reasoning modes (for Q1.2 of Reviewer `kCu9`, W1 of Reviewer `NdeY`, W1,2 & Q1,4 of Reviewer `Yq9o`, W11 & Q9 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * B.6.2 discusses the scalability of the AdaVaR training framework (for Q1.2 of Reviewer `kCu9`, W1 & Q1 of Reviewer `Yq9o`, W11 & Q9 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * B.6.3 discusses extensions to additional modalities (for W34 & Q32 of Reviewer `n9fj`) and languages (for W40 & Q38 of Reviewer `n9fj`)

    * We have added a new section, **Appendix B.7**, to present **several supplementary ablation studies**:

      * In B.7.1, we validate the rationale for using a 1:1 ratio of the two reasoning modes in SFT (for W4 of Reviewer `Yq9o`, W1 & Q1 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In B.7.2, we show that different formats of the mode prefix have negligible impact on the MoVT framework (for W3 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In B.7.3, we investigate the necessity of the SFT in mode learning and the sensitivity to system prompt (for W2 & Q2 of Reviewer `kCu9`)
      * In B.7.4, we verify the necessity of the easy-to-hard data ordering for curriculum learning (for W35 & Q33 of Reviewer `n9fj`);
      * In B.7.5, we demonstrate the robustness of our method to image noise (for W21 & Q19 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

    * The original Appendix B.5 about **case analysis** is **expanded to** **Appendix B.8** in the updated version, where we have supplemented two cases that focus on commonsense reasoning (for Q4 of Reviewer `kCu9`)

3.  We have streamlined the citations that originally contained excessively long author lists (for W23 & Q21 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

4.  We have added clarification in Algorithm 1 regarding the order of the reward list (for W25 & Q23 of Reviewer `n9fj`).

5.  We have **included a link to the anonymous codebase** in the reproducibility statement (for all reviewers to check the implementation of our work).

6.  To avoid disrupting the numbering of figures and prevent confusion for reviewers, the newly added figures are currently appended at the end of the submission version. We will reorganize and renumber them appropriately after the review process is completed.

Above all, we thank all reviewers for the valuable suggestions, which have greatly helped improve the quality of this paper. We also warmly welcome any further feedback or recommendations.
